{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-51054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-51054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-51054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4c9639d528e09f5aa84dd5d9e426387f6297aa76a0dec5fae5b2968fee7f77b"} {"question" "q-51054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-51054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)\\ne f(-1)"] ["math" "{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)"] ["text" "$f(-1)$ does not exist"] ["text" "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function"] ["text" "${lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-51054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8,$ then what is the value of $\\sqrt{f(-1)+1}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-1)={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{f(-1)+1} & =\\sqrt{8+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4c9639d528e09f5aa84dd5d9e426387f6297aa76a0dec5fae5b2968fee7f77b"} {"question" "q-71068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=2,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2} & ={({4}^{2}-6(2))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(16-12)}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4c9639d528e09f5aa84dd5d9e426387f6297aa76a0dec5fae5b2968fee7f77b"} {"question" "q-71071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-2x+3, & x<2 \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=2(2)-3=1,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2x+3) \\\\  & =-2(2)+3 \\\\  & =-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x-3) \\\\  & =2(2)-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist. $\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4c9639d528e09f5aa84dd5d9e426387f6297aa76a0dec5fae5b2968fee7f77b"} {"question" "q-71080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)"] ["text" "$f(2)$ does not exist"] ["text" "${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist"] ["text" "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] ["text" "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-71080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-342242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6$ and $f(6)=14,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1}=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-342242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=f(6)=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1} & =\\sqrt{14+{6}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-342242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4c9639d528e09f5aa84dd5d9e426387f6297aa76a0dec5fae5b2968fee7f77b"} {"question" "q-342242" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"} {"question" "q-342242" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039941/state.json" "file_sha256" "734459424423753d7acd68df3efbc3584478ea074469f99dd83c59f117212873"}] "usage" {"q-51054" []} "basis" 1390 "retractions" [[17592186064804 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x =-1$?\n$f(x) = {x^{3} + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 1 \\\\ x^{2} + 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 1$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186063335 17592186063336 17592186063337 17592186063338 17592186063339] "immutable_fields" [[17592186063334 :answer-field/correct] [17592186063334 :answer-field/id] [17592186063334 :answer-field/key] [17592186063334 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
